  <!-- SECTION 3: AWS OIDC Federation -->
  <section id="oidc-architecture" class="case-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span>03 / IAM SECURITY</span>
      <span class="evidence-tag">ZERO LONG-LIVED SECRETS</span>
    </div>
    <h2>AWS OIDC Federation: Eliminating Long-Lived Credentials</h2>
    <p>
      Traditional CI/CD pipelines store static <code>AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID</code> and <code>AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY</code> tokens in GitHub repository settings. If compromised, attackers obtain persistent cloud access. This platform adopts <strong>GitHub Actions OpenID Connect (OIDC)</strong> web identity federation:
    </p>

    <div class="terminal-window">
      <div class="terminal-header">
        <div class="terminal-dots">
          <span class="terminal-dot red"></span>
          <span class="terminal-dot yellow"></span>
          <span class="terminal-dot green"></span>
        </div>
        <div class="terminal-title">terraform/oidc.tf — IAM Web Identity Trust Policy</div>
      </div>
      <pre><code class="terminal-body"><span class="term-dim"># Restrict STS role assumption strictly to GitHub Actions token issued for this repo:</span>
<span class="term-cyan">data</span> <span class="term-green">"aws_iam_policy_document"</span> <span class="term-yellow">"github_oidc_assume_role"</span> {
  statement {
    effect  = <span class="term-green">"Allow"</span>
    actions = [<span class="term-green">"sts:AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity"</span>]

    principals {
      type        = <span class="term-green">"Federated"</span>
      identifiers = [aws_iam_openid_connect_provider.github.arn]
    }

    condition {
      test     = <span class="term-green">"StringEquals"</span>
      variable = <span class="term-green">"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:aud"</span>
      values   = [<span class="term-green">"sts.amazonaws.com"</span>]
    }

    condition {
      test     = <span class="term-green">"StringLike"</span>
      variable = <span class="term-green">"token.actions.githubusercontent.com:sub"</span>
      values   = [<span class="term-green">"repo:jenidevops30/aws-devsecops-pipeline:*"</span>]
    }
  }
}</code></pre>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: 15px;">
      GitHub Actions requests a cryptographic JSON Web Token (JWT) directly from GitHub's OIDC provider. AWS STS validates the signature, confirms that the repository name matches <code>jenidevops30/aws-devsecops-pipeline</code>, and issues ephemeral credentials valid for a maximum of 1 hour.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- SECTION 4: In-Code Defensive AppSec -->
  <section id="monolith-appsec" class="case-section">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span>04 / APPLICATION SECURITY</span>
      <span class="evidence-tag">DEFENSE-IN-DEPTH</span>
    </div>
    <h2>In-Code AppSec & Defensive Engineering</h2>
    <p>
      The monolith (Order & Inventory Management API) incorporates defense-in-depth directly into application code, ensuring security boundaries are enforced even if perimeter defenses are bypassed:
    </p>

    <div class="appsec-grid">
      <div class="appsec-card">
        <h4>🛡️ Zero-Dependency SQLite</h4>
        <p>
          Uses Node.js 24's native <code>node:sqlite</code> (<code>DatabaseSync</code>). Eliminates external native C++ compilation modules (node-gyp, python buildtools), resulting in a lean, minimal Alpine container with 0 OS CVEs.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="appsec-card">
        <h4>🔒 Parameterized SQL (CWE-89)</h4>
        <p>
          All queries execute via prepared statements (<code>db.prepare("... WHERE id = ?").get(id)</code>). User inputs are strictly treated as data parameters, neutralizing SQL injection attempts automatically.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="appsec-card">
        <h4>🧱 Schema Ingestion (Zod)</h4>
        <p>
          Every incoming payload is parsed and validated by strict Zod schemas before hitting business logic. Malformed payloads or prototype pollution attempts trigger an immediate HTTP 400 rejection.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="appsec-card">
        <h4>👤 Non-Root Unprivileged User</h4>
        <p>
          Container runtime drops root immediately using <code>USER 10001:10001</code>. If an application remote code execution vulnerability were ever discovered, attackers cannot escape container boundaries or modify root filesystem assets.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

